[Line "5.Nf3 b5 6.Bd3 Bb7 7.O-O Nd7 8.a4 b4 9.Ne2 c5 10.c3"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6 with Nf3"]
[Summary "White develops normally and keeps the bishop on d3. Black expands with ...b4 and ...c5; the exchange on c3 leaves White with an isolated a-pawn and a weak c3, and Black has ...Ngf6 and pressure on e4."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. Nf3 { The flexible developing move. It reaches the same position as 4.Nf3 a6 5.Be3. }
( 5. a4 b6 { When White prevents ...b5 with a4, the pawn goes to b6 instead. The bishop still goes to b7 and the structure is a calm
Hippo-style one. } 6. Qd2 Bb7 7. f3 Nd7 )
5... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Gc8b7] } 6. Bd3 { Now e4 is covered, so ...b4 does not win a pawn. } 6... Bb7 7. O-O Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gc7c5] The same set-up as against the 150 Attack:
the queen's knight to d7, the king's knight at home. } 8. a4 { [%cal Ga4b5] Challenges the pawn on b5. Black may play ...b4, and this is the route that costs White the c3-knight's best square. }
8... b4 9. Ne2 { Retreats to the centre; the knight will be able to go to g3 and cover e4. } ( 9. Nd5 Bxd5 10. exd5 Ngf6 { The knight takes on d5 and Black wins a pawn: the white pawn on d5 is
attacked by the knight and has no good defender. } ) 9... c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gb7e4] Strikes at the centre while the knight is still on e2. The pawn on d4 is attacked by c5 and g7, and White must resolve it. }
10. c3 { White attacks the b4-pawn and tries to open lines before ...c4 or ...Ngf6 comes. } 10... bxc3 11. bxc3 Qc7 { [%cal Gg8f6,Gf6e4,Gb7e4] The queen eyes c3 and the pawn on e5 break, and ...Ngf6 comes next, adding a second attacker to e4.
White has a weak c3-pawn and an isolated a4-pawn. He must defend e4 with Ng3 (12.Qc2? runs into ...c4, and the bishop on d3, blocked by the knight on e2, has no good retreat). The study gives White a slight edge, but Black has
real play in the centre and on the queenside in return. } *

[Line "5.Nf3 b5 6.a3 Bb7 7.Bd3 Nd7 8.O-O c5 9.Re1 cxd4 10.Bxd4 e5"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6 with Nf3"]
[Summary "White spends a tempo on a3 to stop ...b4. Black plays ...c5 anyway, opens the position and answers Bxd4 with ...e5, getting a Dragon-like centre with pressure on e4."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. Nf3 b5 6. a3 { Stops ...b4, but it costs a tempo and does nothing for the centre. Compare with the previous game, where the pawn went to a4 only when it was needed. }
6... Bb7 7. Bd3 Nd7 8. O-O c5 { [%cal Gc5d4] Black does not wait. } 9. Re1 { Defends e4 once more; 9.Qd2 cxd4 10.Bxd4 is the other main try. } 9... cxd4 10. Bxd4 e5!? { [%cal Ge5d4,Gb7e4,Gg8f6]
The ambitious reply, and the one Hillarp Persson favours. The bishop is attacked and has to retreat to e3, and Black gets a pawn centre with the a1-h8 diagonal closed for the moment. The bishop on b7 and the knight
on f6 will hit e4 together. }
( 10... Ngf6?! { Natural, but 11.e5 opens the position at the right moment for White. The structural advantage that Black has gained (the d6/e7 pawns against an unopposed e-pawn) is gone. } 11. e5 )
( 10... Bxd4 { Simpler. } 11. Nxd4 Ngf6 { Also good and simpler: Black trades the bishop that was attacking his position, develops with tempo on e4 and keeps a healthy structure. } )
11. Be3 { The bishop cannot stay: after ...exd4 it would be lost, and Bxe5 loses it to ...dxe5. } 11... Ngf6 { [%cal Gf6e4,Gb7e4] Two pieces attack e4, and White cannot move the bishop on d3 without losing the pawn. } 12. Qd2 O-O { The king is safe: the
centre is blocked, so there is no quick opening for White. } 13. Rad1 Rc8 { [%cal Gc8c3] The rook takes the open c-file and hits the knight on c3, which only the b2-pawn and the queen defend. }
14. Bg5 Qb6 { [%cal Gb6b2,Gb6d4] The queen hits b2 and d4 and Black can answer 15.Bf1 with the exchange sacrifice ...Rxc3, as the study game did. A
dynamic position with equal chances and the more active pieces for Black. } *

[Line "Second option: 4...Nf6 5.f3 c6 6.Qd2 b5 7.O-O-O Nbd7 8.Kb1 Qa5"]
[Section "4.Be3 Nf6: the Pirc second option"]
[Summary "A Czech-Pirc set-up against the 150 Attack. Black plays ...c6, ...b5, ...Nbd7 and ...Qa5, delays short castling and counterattacks on the queenside."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 Nf6 { The Pirc order. It can be chosen when White has shown his hand: 4...Nf6 is as common as 4...a6 in the elite games, and the structures are
close relatives. The price is that Bh6 and the pawn storm now have a target, so Black must handle his king with care. } 5. f3 { The 150 Attack proper. } ( 5. Qd2 c6 6. f3 b5 { Transposes. } )
5... c6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gd8a5,Gb8d7] The Czech-Pirc move, and the one the study recommends against the 150 Attack: ...c6 prepares ...b5 and ...Qa5, and it keeps the king in the centre for now. The study stresses:
against this attack, delay short castling. After 5...O-O 6.Qd2 White has a famous attacking pattern with Bh6, h4-h5 and Qg5, and Black has to know it exactly. }
6. Qd2 b5 { [%cal Gb5b4] } 7. O-O-O { White commits his king to the queenside, where Black is about to attack. } 7... Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7b6,Gd7e5] Develops without blocking the queen's route and prepares ...Qa5. From d7
the knight also covers b6 and e5, and it supports ...b4. } 8. Kb1 { Takes the king off the c-file, where a queen on a5 would otherwise be annoying.
Other tries: 8.h4 h5 9.Nh3 and 8.Bh6 Bxh6 9.Qxh6 Qa5. } 8... Qa5 { [%cal Ga5c3,Gb5b4] The queen hits c3 and a2 and prepares ...b4, which would drive the knight from c3 and weaken e4. Both kings are on the queenside and the pawn storms run
in opposite directions: Black's on the queenside, White's with g4 and h4 on the other wing. Unbalanced and with chances for both; the book scores are nearly even, and Black's king can still choose between castling short and staying put. } *
